<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;QPSK_Xparam_RFLink&amp;nbsp;Example&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies Example Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;FONT face=Arial&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;What you need to know&amp;nbsp;before executing this example&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; How to create and&amp;nbsp;execute a Data Flow simulation in SystemVue.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;What&amp;nbsp;does this example do&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; This example contains two Data Flow simulation schematics, namely, &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;QPSK&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; and &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;QPSK_VSA&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Both of them demonstrate &lt;STRONG&gt;how to bring&lt;/STRONG&gt; &lt;STRONG&gt;an actual RF device&lt;/STRONG&gt; (in this example, an RF Amplifier) &lt;STRONG&gt;into a system level simulation&lt;/STRONG&gt; &lt;STRONG&gt;in SystemVue through&amp;nbsp;the&amp;nbsp;X-parameters characterization&lt;/STRONG&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;STRONG&gt;of the RF device &lt;/STRONG&gt;(&quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#000080&gt;AMP_XParams.mdf&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; file in this example).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Observe the simulation results of&amp;nbsp;constellation in&amp;nbsp;the two&amp;nbsp;plots: &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;DF1_S_Orig,&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; &lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;DF1_S_RFLink&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT color=#000000&gt;, and the measured EVM values:&amp;nbsp;RF_LINK_EVM_RMS, REF_EVM_RMS (values are in %) in &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;DF1_Data&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Please note that the only difference bewteen the two schematics is that the &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;QPSK_VSA&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; design relies on Keysight 89600 VSA software to perform the QPSK demodulation and EVM computation, hence it requires the installation of Keysight 89600 VSA software on your computer.&amp;nbsp; The &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#000080&gt;QPSK_EVM.set&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; setup file is used to set up the VSA.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Theory of Operation&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Both schematics generate QPSK modulated RF signal as testing stimulus&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;.&amp;nbsp; This RF signal will be demodulated directly along one path; while along the other path, it is&amp;nbsp;demodulated after passing through an RF_Link that embodies an &quot;RF amplifier&quot;&amp;nbsp;via its X-parameters profile.&amp;nbsp; The amplifier is intentionally pushed into compression, hence its impact on the EVM and constellation of the QPSK signal is clearly observable in the two constellation plots: &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;DF1_S_Orig&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; and &lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;DF1_S_RFLink&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT color=#000000&gt;, and the two EVM measured values:&amp;nbsp;RF_LINK_EVM_RMS, REF_EVM_RMS (values are in %) in &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#aa0000&gt;DF1_Data&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Further Exploration&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;In the following, we will show you step-by-step how to&amp;nbsp;create&amp;nbsp;a Data Flow schematic that uses X-parameters profile&amp;nbsp;of an RF device.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;We will also show you how to generate a Spectrasys simulation to analyze RF device characteristics in the&amp;nbsp;frequency domain (i.e. harmonics, mixing terms, etc.).&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;The X-parameters profile can be extracted&amp;nbsp;either in ADS from its design or through Keysight network analyzers that supports X-parameters measurement.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;1. Create a Data Flow schematic that incorporates the X-parameters profile of an RF device&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;FONT color=#808080&gt;&amp;nbsp;Note that&amp;nbsp;you can create your Data Flow schematic before the sub-network that embodies the X-parameter. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL dir=ltr&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Create an sub-network that embodies the X-parameters profile&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Mouse right click on the &quot;Designs&quot; folder and &lt;STRONG&gt;Add -&amp;gt; Designs -&amp;gt;Add Sub-Network Model ...&lt;/STRONG&gt; and give it a meaningful name.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Go to the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#500070&gt;Part Selector A&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#800040&gt;window&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; and select &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;RF Design&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; as &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff8200&gt;Current Library&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&quot;, leave &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff8200&gt;Category&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&quot; at its default &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&amp;lt;All&amp;gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot;. Now at almost the bottom of the list of the parts, you will see &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;X-parameters&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; part, click it and then click any where in the sub-network empty design schematic.&amp;nbsp; An X-parameters part will be dropped into the design schematic.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Double click the dropped part and use the &quot;Browse...&quot; button next to the &quot;Filename (data source):&quot; field to provide the X-parameters file.&amp;nbsp; Click OK and the appropriate number of connection pins/ports will be available now. (&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#c80080&gt;Optional&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;: if there is a need to connect the Ground of the part, check the &quot;Show Reference Ground on Symbol&quot; checkbox).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;If any of the pins/ports are DC bias&amp;nbsp;pins/ports,&amp;nbsp;you&amp;nbsp;can&amp;nbsp;add the DC&amp;nbsp;bias here too.&amp;nbsp;You can use either &lt;STRONG&gt;Signal&amp;nbsp;Ground&lt;/STRONG&gt; part or &lt;STRONG&gt;Source(DC Volt)&lt;/STRONG&gt; part&amp;nbsp;to provide DC bias.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;You can also add R, L, C components in this sub-network.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Add &lt;STRONG&gt;Input&lt;/STRONG&gt; and &lt;STRONG&gt;Output&lt;/STRONG&gt; parts to the desired pins/ports to provide input and output interfaces for this sub-network design.&amp;nbsp; Feel free to&amp;nbsp;use meaningful&amp;nbsp;names for these &lt;STRONG&gt;Input&lt;/STRONG&gt; and &lt;STRONG&gt;Output&lt;/STRONG&gt; parts.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Create a Data Flow schematic&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI dir=ltr&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Bring the sub-network into the Data Flow schematic&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Drag the sub-network icon from the workspace tree into the Data Flow&amp;nbsp;schematic, notice a RF_Link part is automatically created for you as a access interface for the underlying sub-network design.&amp;nbsp; Double click on this RF_Link part to see how it&amp;nbsp;wraps around the sub-network you just created.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;DIV style=&quot;MARGIN-RIGHT: 0px&quot;&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Now create a Data Flow simulation controller, i.e. Data Flow Analysis, and configure it to simulate the Data Flow schematic you just completed.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/DIV&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;2. &lt;STRONG&gt;Create a Spectrasys simulation to analyze the RF device characteristics in the frequency domain&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Create a sub-network that embodies the X-parameters profile&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Create a Spectrasys design schematic&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Mouse right click on the &quot;Designs&quot; folder and &lt;STRONG&gt;Add -&amp;gt; Designs -&amp;gt;Add Schematic... &lt;/STRONG&gt;to add a new design schematic&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Bring in&amp;nbsp;an RF signals source into the design schematic by&amp;nbsp;dragging a&amp;nbsp;&quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Source (Multi)&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; part from the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#500070&gt;Part Selector A&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#800040&gt;window&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt; with &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;RF Design&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; selected as &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff8200&gt;Current Library&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&quot; - Note: &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#ff0000&gt;always bring in some &lt;FONT color=#000000&gt;RF Design&lt;/FONT&gt; parts before&amp;nbsp;step 4 below&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Double click the placed &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Source (Multi)&quot;&lt;/STRONG&gt; part and set it up.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Drag the sub-network design icon from the workspace tree into this schematic and connect its input to the place &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Source (Multi&lt;/STRONG&gt;)&quot; (&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#c80080&gt;Optional&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;: To remove the Ground pin/port from the placed part, double click on the part and click the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#c80080&gt;Advanced Options...&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; button,&amp;nbsp;under the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=#c80080&gt;Symbol&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; tab change the content in the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Symbol&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&quot; field from &quot;2-PORT&quot; to &quot;2-PORTNOGND&quot;).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Add an &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Output&quot;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&amp;nbsp;part to the output of the placed sub-network part&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Add a Spectrasys simulation controller&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Mouse right click on the &quot;Designs&quot; folder and &lt;STRONG&gt;Add -&amp;gt;Analyses -&amp;gt; Add RF System Analysis...&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Select the appropriate design&amp;nbsp;for the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Design to Simulate&lt;/STRONG&gt;:&quot; field&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Click the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Calculate Now&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; button.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/OL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Analyze the simulation results&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;LI&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;Go to the RF design schematic you just simulated, mouse right click on the &quot;&lt;STRONG&gt;Output&lt;/STRONG&gt;&quot; part, &lt;STRONG&gt;Add New Graph/Table -&amp;gt; ... New Power Plot ...&lt;/STRONG&gt; This will create a spectral lines plot.&amp;nbsp; Now move the mouse around those spectral lines and you can see their values.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/LI&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&lt;/UL&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&lt;FONT size=2&gt;For your convenience, a complete Spectrasys simulation has been created (see System1 analysis under Analyses folder and DCP_vs_Pin and System1_PWR_at_Node_2 graphs in the Graphs folder).&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/FONT&gt;</Text>
